\details{
\subsection{Reading a structural pattern}{

A pattern describes connected residues in a glycan graph.
Read a path from left to right, towards the reducing end.
Separate adjacent residues with \code{-}: for example,
\code{Gal-GlcNAc} asks for a Gal attached directly to a GlcNAc. It does not skip
intervening residues. Without end anchors, this path can occur within a
larger glycan and does not have to describe the whole structure.

Start with residue names, then add constraints as needed:
\itemize{
\item \code{Gal} matches a galactose residue.
\item \code{.} matches any one residue, not any number of residues.
\item \code{Hex} and \code{HexNAc} match members of the corresponding glyrepr classes;
for example, \code{Hex} matches Gal, Glc, and Man.
\item \code{Sia} matches Neu5Ac, Neu5Gc, and Kdn.
\item \verb{[Gal|Man]} matches either Gal or Man. Alternatives can also be paths,
as in \verb{[Gal-GlcNAc|Gal-GalNAc]}.
\item \verb{[^Fuc]} matches one residue that is not Fuc. Negated alternatives must
each describe a single residue; \verb{[^Fuc|Xyl]} excludes both Fuc and Xyl.
}
}

\subsection{Specifying linkages and the reducing end}{

A linkage belongs to the residue on its non-reducing side. For example,
\code{Galb4-GlcNAc} requires Gal to be attached to GlcNAc by a beta1-4 linkage.
Use glyco-regex linkage suffixes such as \code{Galb4}; IUPAC-style
linkages such as \code{Gal(b1-4)} are not supported in patterns.
The suffix specifies the anomer and acceptor position. The donor position
is 1, or 2 for Neu5Ac, Neu5Gc, Kdn, and \code{Sia}.

Omitted linkage fields impose no constraint. Thus \code{Gal-GlcNAc} does not
specify a linkage, and \code{GlcNAca} specifies only the alpha anomer, either
at an internal residue or at the reducing end. A specified acceptor
position, as in \code{GlcNAca4}, requires an outgoing glycosidic linkage towards
the reducing end and cannot match the reducing-end residue itself.

To require reducing-end GlcNAc with an alpha anomer, use \verb{GlcNAca$}.

Unknown linkage fields (\verb{?}) act as wildcards in either the pattern or
the input glycan, following glycowork. A specified linkage can therefore
match an input whose corresponding field is unknown; a match establishes
compatibility, not certainty about that linkage. Ambiguous positions
match when their possible values overlap, so acceptor \code{3/4} is compatible
with \code{4} but not \code{6}.
}

\subsection{Requiring or excluding branches}{

Ordinary parentheses describe a sibling branch attached to the next
residue in the main path. In \code{Galb4-(Fuca3)-GlcNAc}, both Gal and Fuc
attach to the same GlcNAc: Gal by beta1-4 and Fuc by alpha1-3. The Fuc
is not inserted between Gal and GlcNAc. Required branch residues are part
of the match. Additional branches are allowed unless explicitly excluded.
Pattern parentheses serve a different purpose from the square brackets
used for branches in an input IUPAC string such as
\verb{Gal(b1-4)[Fuc(a1-3)]GlcNAc}.

Use \verb{(!...-)} to require the absence of a sibling branch. For example,
\verb{Galb4-(!Fuc-)GlcNAc} matches the Gal-GlcNAc path only if the GlcNAc has
no remaining Fuc branch with any linkage. \verb{Galb4-(!Fuca3-)GlcNAc} excludes
only a branch compatible with alpha1-3 Fuc. The check considers branches
not already used by the preceding pattern and can describe several
residues, as in \verb{Gal-(!Gal-GlcNAc-)Man}.

Keep the attachment dash inside the absence group, immediately before
\verb{)}, and write the attachment residue directly after it. Bare \code{!Fuc},
\verb{[!Fuc]}, and absence groups without the final dash are rejected.

Unlike \verb{[^Fuc]}, which matches and includes one non-Fuc residue,
\verb{(!Fuc-)} only checks a condition and adds no residues to the match.
Consequently, \verb{[^Fuc]-GlcNAc} requires a non-Fuc residue before GlcNAc;
\verb{(!Fuc-)GlcNAc} can match GlcNAc alone if it has no Fuc branch.
}

\subsection{Grouping and repetition}{

Use square brackets or \verb{(?:...)} to group a path. Ordinary parentheses
mean a branch, not a path group. Attach a repetition operator to the group:
\itemize{
\item \verb{[Gal]?}: zero or one Gal.
\item \verb{[Gal]*}: zero or more consecutive Gal residues.
\item \verb{[Gal]+}: one or more consecutive Gal residues.
\item \verb{[Gal]\{2\}}: exactly two consecutive Gal residues.
\item \verb{[Gal]\{2,4\}}: between two and four consecutive Gal residues.
\item \verb{[Gal]\{2,\}}: at least two consecutive Gal residues.
\item \verb{[Gal]\{,4\}}: zero to four consecutive Gal residues.
}

A group can contain more than one residue: \verb{[Galb4-GlcNAcb3]\{2\}} repeats
the two-residue path twice. Use grouped syntax for repetition rather than
forms such as \verb{Gal*}, which are rejected.

Repetition is greedy by default: longer repetitions are tried first.
Append \verb{?} to try shorter repetitions first, as in \verb{[Gal]+?}. In either
case, the rest of the pattern must also match. Open upper bounds are
limited by the input graph, not a fixed repeat count. A pattern that
permits zero repetitions never produces an empty match.
}

\subsection{Anchors and lookaround assertions}{

Assertions restrict where a match is allowed without adding residues:
\itemize{
\item \verb{^Gal} requires Gal at a non-reducing terminus (a residue with no
further residues attached on its non-reducing side).
\item \verb{GlcNAc$} requires GlcNAc at the reducing end.
\item \verb{Gal\%} requires Gal to be internal: neither a non-reducing terminus
nor the reducing-end residue.
}

Lookaround checks a neighbouring path without including it in the result.
Lookahead examines the reducing-end direction: \verb{Gal-(?=GlcNAc)} matches
Gal only when the next residue is GlcNAc, whereas \code{Gal-(?!GlcNAc)} requires
that the next residue does not match GlcNAc (or is absent). Lookbehind
examines the non-reducing direction: \verb{(?<=Gal-)GlcNAc} matches GlcNAc
when a Gal is attached to it, and \verb{(?<!Gal-)GlcNAc} requires that no such
Gal path exists. Paths inside lookbehind are still written towards the
reducing end, just like the rest of the pattern.

Lookaround, branch-absence checks, and anchors cannot be repeated.
Assertions alone do not produce a match because every returned match
must contain at least one residue.
}

\subsection{Capturing part of a match}{

Use \verb{(?<name>...)} to give a matched path a name. For example,
\verb{(?<terminal>Gal)-GlcNAc} matches the two-residue path and captures its Gal
as \code{terminal}. \code{\link[=rex_match]{rex_match()}} and \code{\link[=rex_match_all]{rex_match_all()}} return the full match
in the \code{match} column and captured fragments in their named columns.
This named-capture syntax is an extension to glycowork.

Capture names must start with a letter, contain only letters, digits,
or underscores, and be unique within the pattern. The name \code{match} is
reserved. Use \verb{(?:...)} when grouping without a named capture is desired.
See \code{\link[=rex_detect]{rex_detect()}} for how matches are ordered and counted, including
overlapping matches and matches contained within larger ones.
}
